# Verdict — Gate (b): the paper's WRITTEN distance-only channel does NOT de-collapse the baseline

**Date:** 2026-06-16 · **Status:** COLLAPSED (decisive, both weight rows) ·
**Scope:** baseline FOUNDATION faithfulness gate only — **READINESS-ONLY, no
effectiveness / beats-baseline / Pareto claim.** · **Prereg:**
`paper_baseline/PREREG-phaseb-distance.md` (frozen, data-blind).

**Paper:** Y. Sun et al., *IEEE Comm. Lett.* 28(12), Dec. 2024 (`PAP-2024-MORL-MULTIBEAM`).
**Authority:** `fable/baseline-repro-master-plan.md` §"2026-06-16 UPDATE" (the
FAITHFULNESS GATE that must be settled before Stream C/D).
**Builds on:** Phase-1 (`…-phase1-verdict.md`, COLLAPSED — literal channel, no
per-beam term) and Phase-2 (`…-phase2-verdict.md`, antenna directivity de-collapses
at θ_h≤1.0°).

---

## TL;DR

Phase-2 de-collapsed the baseline by adding a per-beam **antenna directivity**
`G(θ)`. But the paper's WRITTEN channel `G_{i,l,v}=FSPL(d_{i,l,v})·A(d_{i,l,v})·η`
(Eq. 1, lines 119–132) has **no antenna pattern** — it indexes `d_{i,l,v}` by beam
but its text defines it as "the distance between **satellite and user**." Gate (b)
takes that `(l,v)` index literally in the most charitable faithful way — each beam
gets a genuinely per-beam **footprint-centre distance** `d_{i,l,v}=√(h²+‖u_i−f_{l,v}‖²)`
through the paper's own Eq. 1, **shared Rician, NO antenna pattern** — and asks:
**does the paper's own distance-only written model differentiate a satellite's V=7
beams enough to reproduce a spread (working) baseline?**

**Result: NO — it COLLAPSES, decisively, at both weight rows.** Trained for the
paper's 9000 episodes (`algorithms/modqn.py` byte-unchanged), the greedy policy lands
all 100 users on **one beam at every checkpoint** (`active_beam_count_mean = 1.00`,
`modal_frac_mean = 1.000`; **MAX active over the entire run = 1.00**, both rows). The
distance channel separates a satellite's 7 beams by only **~0.42 dB** (best-vs-2nd
margin **~0.055 dB**) — about **60× weaker** than the Phase-2 antenna taper (tens of
dB at θ_h≤1.0°) — so the per-beam signal is buried and the collapse is immediate and
total (collapsed already at ep 100, ε≈0.99, unlike even the *collapsing* Phase-2
θ_h=2.0 case which started spread at ~active 9).

**Conclusion (the gate's answer):** the paper's WRITTEN distance-only channel is
**insufficient** to differentiate intra-satellite beams → **Phase-2's per-beam
antenna directivity is a NECESSARY, DISCLOSED deviation**, not an optional one, and a
**finding**: the letter under-specifies the intra-satellite beam model (its Eq. 1, read
literally, makes a satellite's V beams ~channel-identical). The distance-type model is
**NOT faithfully sufficient to spread** — the Phase-2 antenna deviation is kept and
**re-labelled "disclosed deviation," not "faithful."**

## Results (greedy ε=0 eval, paper aggregation `Σrj = Σ_t mean_i r_j`; eval seeds 100/200/300/400/500)

| variant | weights | **Σr1** | Σr2 | Σr3 | **active_beam** | modal_frac | MAX active (whole run) | verdict |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **(b) distance-only** | [0.5,0.3,0.2] | **1.298** | −2.000 | −1.298 | **1.000** | 1.000 | **1.00** | **COLLAPSED** |
| **(b) distance-only** | [1,1,1] | **1.280** | −2.000 | −1.280 | **1.000** | 1.000 | **1.00** | **COLLAPSED** |
| Phase-1 (no per-beam) | [0.5,0.3,0.2] | 0.91 | −2.0 | −0.91 | 1.00 | 1.000 | 1.00 | COLLAPSED |
| Phase-2 antenna θ_h=1.0° | [0.5,0.3,0.2] | 13.42 | −1.51 | −1.64 | **13.36** | 0.192 | (spread) | SPREAD |
| paper Table II | [0.5,0.3,0.2] | 32.96 | −7.66 | −2.91 | (>1) | (≪1) | — | (spread) |

Both (b) arms **early-killed** at ep ≈ 7100 (collapse confirmed stable in the
ε-floor); train ≈ 593–599 s/arm (single-thread BLAS). The de-collapse that Phase-2
achieved with the **antenna** does **not** occur with **distance alone**.

### The clincher: ε-greedy training reward tracks the exploration fraction (as in Phase-1)

| ε | ~1.0 | ~0.30 | ~0.15 | ~0.08 | ~0.01 |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| (b) ε-greedy train r1 ([0.5,0.3,0.2]) | **32.51** | 22.58 | 14.65 | 9.60 | **2.32** |

Σr1≈33 is again the **random-assignment** level (each beam carries its own bandwidth →
random spreading is near-optimal), achieved at ε≈1 and decaying monotonically to the
greedy-collapse value as exploration vanishes. As in Phase-1, **the learned policy is
worse than random** — the distance heterogeneity does not give the shared net a basin
to learn a spread policy. (`[1,1,1]` identical: 32.51 → 2.26.)

## Mechanism (data-blind probe `phaseb_distance_probe.py`, pre-run)

| | intra-sat per-beam SNR spread (dB) | best-vs-2nd margin (dB) | best-SNR #unique(users) | untrained-greedy #unique | served SNR med (dB) |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **Phase-1 (no per-beam)** | **0.000** (beams identical) | 0.000 | 2.0 | **1.0** | 13.7 |
| **(b) distance ON** | med **0.421** (max 0.48) | **0.055** | 3.0 | **1.0** | 13.7 |
| *(ref)* Phase-2 θ_h=2.0 antenna | tens of dB | large | 16.3 | 2.0–2.7 → *still collapsed* | 11.9 |

Why distance is too weak: a satellite's V=7 beam footprints sit on a 45-km hex ring;
against the **780 km** slant the 45–90 km footprint offsets change the FSPL by only
~0.4 dB, and the shared per-(user,sat) Rician fade (≈ K=20 dB → a few tenths of a dB)
is **comparable to the entire per-beam signal**. The untrained shared net's greedy
argmax is already `#unique=1.0` — the **same collapse signature as Phase-1**, and
weaker than even Phase-2's θ_h=2.0 probe (which gave 2.0–2.7 yet still collapsed after
training). The best-SNR-per-user reference itself reaches only **3** unique beams
(modal 0.76): the distance channel cannot produce a spread assignment *even by
construction*, let alone learn one.

## What this is / is NOT

- **IS** the answer to the master-plan FAITHFULNESS GATE: the paper's **own
  distance-only written channel does not reproduce a working (spread) baseline.** The
  basic question — "is the paper's baseline faithfully reproducible from its written
  model?" — is settled **negatively** for the channel as literally written.
- **IS** a third independent re-confirmation of the **shared-Q-homogenization** collapse
  driver (Phase-1 root cause, fable Step-3 *flat-Q*, Arm-0a): near-homogeneous per-user
  channel → identical argmax → pile-up; only a **strong** per-user contrast (the θ_h≤1.0°
  antenna) holds spread through training.
- **IS** the justification to **re-label Phase-2 "faithful" → "disclosed deviation"**:
  the antenna directivity is necessary (distance alone fails) and is correct physics the
  letter under-specified, but it is **not in the paper's written channel** → it is a
  **disclosed, physically-motivated DEVIATION**, drawn neutrally as a Layer-0 (ENV)
  attribute (DISCLOSE ≠ EMPHASIZE).
- **IS NOT** a beats-baseline / effectiveness / Pareto claim (READINESS-ONLY).
- **IS NOT** an adversarial "the paper is wrong" claim. Per the master plan's SAFE
  FRAMING this stays **internal** (repo/notes): the working baseline FOUNDATION uses the
  disclosed antenna deviation; the thesis does not publish "the baseline collapses."
- **Scale-invariant:** the verdict is read from `active_beam_count_mean`/`modal_frac_mean`
  and the greedy-vs-exploratory gap, independent of the [PS-3]/[PS-4]/[PS-7]
  reward-magnitude reconstruction (B_r = 3.142 = Phase-1's, data-blind round-robin).

## Faithfulness ledger (Phase-1 → Phase-2 → Gate (b))

| variant | per-beam channel | faithful to letter? | baseline |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Phase-1** | none — Eq. 1 literal, V beams of a sat **channel-identical** | **most literal** | COLLAPSED |
| **Gate (b)** | per-beam **distance** to footprint (Eq. 1, `(l,v)` index taken literally), no antenna | **most charitable faithful** | **COLLAPSED** |
| **Phase-2** | per-beam **antenna directivity** `G(θ)` (not in the written channel) | **disclosed DEVIATION** | SPREAD (θ_h≤1.0°) |

The two faithful readings (literal Phase-1, charitable-distance (b)) both collapse;
only the **disclosed deviation** (Phase-2 antenna) spreads. That is the gate's outcome:
**the working baseline requires the disclosed antenna deviation.**

