## 2. The three arms (and why they cannot be stacked)

| Arm | Module | Class | Deploy rule | Learned? | Status |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **baseline** | `algorithms/modqn.py` (via trainers) | `MODQNTrainer` | per-user argmax over shared scalarized Q | yes | the collapse anchor; baseline-protect G1 |
| **coordinated** | `coordinated_multi_catfish/` | `CoordinatedMultiCatfishMODQN` | learned **coordination decoder** (sequential per-user occupancy-aware decode) overriding `select_actions` | yes | BUILT (`509d930`); never run on fixed env |
| **executor** | `global_assign_decode/` | `GlobalAssignDecodeMODQN` | non-learned **channel-surrogate** global assignment (`_coordinate_ascent`) overriding `select_actions` | **no** (policy-independent; never reads Q) | BUILT (`85b84a6`); eval-only |

**Mutual exclusivity (binding, do not violate):** coordinated and executor **both
override `select_actions`** → they are mutually-exclusive deploy decoders. They may
co-exist as **separate ablation arms/cells**, never as one stacked mechanism.

**Knob-trap veto (binding, do not reopen):** "executor reads the learned Q" was
codex-BLOCKed in SDD-06 (C1: pinned-φ + B/N still smuggles a separable knob ≡ argmax,
or needs a post-hoc congestion knob = MR hazard). The executor stays
**policy-independent / checkpoint-invariant** (scorer SHA `eee7c78…`). Do not wire Q
