/home/sat/route-c-wt/docs/research/angle-aware-ee-multicatfish/inspiration/A-marl-coordination.md:22:- **Concrete idea:** **Hysteretic loss** that asymmetrically caps the update magnitude when the TD error is negative: `L = (1/B) Σ β · (δ)²`, with `β = 1` if δ ≥ 0 and `β ∈ (0,1)` if δ < 0 (paper schedules β from 0.2 → 1.0). Plus a **successive Q-network** trick: same network evaluates candidate satellites one at a time (NN output size = 1 instead of M), enabling scaling to mega-constellations.
/home/sat/route-c-wt/docs/research/env-foundation/07-design-coupling-findings.md:35:- **Mechanism:** The G1-fenced hook computes η = rate·G_T·load/P_beam with rate=(B_alloc/load)·log2(1+SINR) ⇒ load cancels EXACTLY: η = B_alloc·log2(1+SINR)·G_T/P_beam. Therefore (i) with fixed per-beam power (the paper's only stated convention, Table I; live tx_power_w demoted DEAD) and live noise, η is bit-identical across loads 1/14/100 — ZERO anti-collapse gradient; (ii) adding D5 (σ²=N₀·W_u): SINR gains 10log10(3N) dB so η strictly INCREASES with beam load — pro-collapse r1, ×1.60 at N=14 and ×2.05 at N=100 vs N=1 (re-verified this session), before interference relief (full collapse also zeroes co-channel interference — f4=0.577 means ~42% of M1 is interference-suppressed under spread); (iii) r2 favors sticky, r3 is numerically invisible (GAP-G <2% share) — no other objective reliably opposes collapse. The only thing currently saving the gradient is the live SDD-04 concave surface P=0.25+0.35·√N (REP-04h, beyond-paper) — which no D1–D7 row decides to keep. Same undeclared choice also controls D2b's 'cap dormant' claim (concave: wake at 571–1003 users/sat — unreachable; BH-lite fixed 10 W/beam as in D2b's own cited refs: K×10 W > 19.95 W ⇒ cap binds ALWAYS, −1.8/−3.0/−4.0 dB at K=3/4/5, moving L2-1/L2-2/L4-5) and D3's EE denominator scale (~3× shift). Structural collapse is the project's documented kill-class (L5-3; ledger §D/G/H) and no L0–L4 gate measures the reward-mode load gradient before training (L4-3's r1-biased arm is max-THROUGHPUT, not max-η).
/home/sat/route-c-wt/docs/research/env-foundation/07-design-coupling-findings.md:46:### [F6] D4b's entire cross-satellite interference regime presupposes an undeclared isotropic-Rx-toward-all-satellites assumption that contradicts the 35 dBi VSAT terminal class D5 itself anchors to — with any declared Rx pattern, cross-sat interference arrives ~25-45 dB down through sidelobes and BOTH D4b option-effect cells are wrong by tens of dB
/home/sat/route-c-wt/docs/research/env-foundation/07-design-coupling-findings.md:49:- **Mechanism:** The live code multiplies the scalar 35 dBi receive gain into EVERY satellite's channel with no angle dependence (serving and interferers alike). A 35 dBi aperture at 20 GHz is physically directive (~3.6° equivalent cone) and points at the SERVING satellite; under D1+D2a the window satellites are separated by tens of degrees as seen from the UE (e.g. el70/az0 vs el45/az90 ⇒ 48.4° separation), so cross-sat co-color interference physically arrives through Rx sidelobes (32−25·log10(48°) ≈ −10 dBi vs +35 dBi ⇒ ~−45 dB). With any declared VSAT Rx pattern, both D4b options collapse toward the intra-satellite regime: option (ii)'s 'strong cross-sat co-channel / serves the interference-must-matter requirement' and option (i)'s 'cleaner spectrum' descriptions become wrong by tens of dB, and the choice 04 frames as 'shapes the interference regime the whole thesis trains on' becomes nearly moot. The 06 ledger pins only a uniform terminal CLASS, not Rx-isotropy-toward-interferers — the package internally asserts the directive terminal whose interference consequence it ignores.
/home/sat/route-c-wt/docs/research/env-foundation/07-design-coupling-findings.md:51:- **Proposed fix:** Add a controlling D4b sub-decision — UE Rx pattern toward non-serving satellites (declared isotropic worst-case vs S.1528/32−25logθ envelope) — and condition both option-effect cells plus the L2-4 re-measurement plan on it before the user selects.
/home/sat/route-c-wt/docs/research/env-foundation/07-design-coupling-findings.md:62:### [F3] D3's geometric-signal rationale is direction-INVERTED: under D2a area-tracking, lower elevation gives SMALLER off-axis angles (θ_max 8.0° at zenith → 3.15° at ε=15°), compressing served G_T spread to ≲1-3 dB — less angle-term variance than the live env the claim says it improves on — while an UNSTATED edge-user incentive (+15 dB G_T vs +8.1 dB FSPL) pushes toward LOW-elevation satellites
/home/sat/route-c-wt/docs/research/env-foundation/07-design-coupling-findings.md:65:- **Mechanism:** 04 D3 claims 'with D2a area-tracking, off-axis angles now vary meaningfully across serving satellites (low-elevation sat ⇒ larger θ within its cluster)' and that the EE term gets 'signal it never had on the single-zenith-sat live env'. The geometry says the opposite: with every cluster boresight on the area center, a UE ≤109.7 km from center subtends θ_max = atan(109.7/d_slant) = 8.0° at zenith (d=780) but 4.1° at ε≈25° and 3.15° at ε=15° (d=1993), with along-range components further compressed by sin ε. For the dominant non-zenith serving population most users sit ≤2° off boresight where the 3.32°-θ_3dB pattern varies ≲1.1 dB — near-constant G_T; the cross-satellite EE driver is FSPL (8.15 dB spread), entering via SINR, not the explicit angle term. The live 2.0°-ring layout actually forces LARGER best-beam angles (up to ~5.3° ⇒ −8.14 dB) — the 'never had' comparison is backwards. The variance that remains comes from (a) ring-vs-center assignment — but ring beams are the degenerate off-area actions (hex-collapse finding) — and (b) an unstated cross-satellite incentive: an area-edge user gains ~+15 dB G_T choosing a low-elevation server against only 8.1 dB extra FSPL ⇒ the angle-aware EE term systematically rewards low-elevation serving — a reward-shaping property the D3 pre-reg / L4-4 recalibration / L4-3 tension expectations must anticipate, and currently mis-anchored.
/home/sat/route-c-wt/docs/research/env-foundation/07-design-coupling-findings.md:67:- **Proposed fix:** Rewrite D3's consequence bullet with the correct sign (angular compression at low elevation; G_T spread ~0..−3 dB; FSPL is the dominant cross-satellite EE driver; explicit low-elevation-preference incentive) and require the D3 pre-reg to record a measured served-θ / G_T distribution from a pointing-aware probe over the LP-2 elevation distribution before freezing reward expectations.
/home/sat/route-c-wt/docs/research/env-foundation/07-design-coupling-findings.md:72:- **Mechanism:** color=(local_beam_idx+sat_idx) mod 3 has only 3 distinct per-sat patterns; any 4-sat window (D1 reading B guarantees ≥4, mean visible 5.43) contains ≥1 identical-pattern pair colliding beam-for-beam at full option-(ii) strength (E[same-offset pairs among 4] = C(4,2)/3 = 2; sat_idx mod 3 over the nearest-4 window is an arbitrary hash uncorrelated with geometry). Non-colliding interferer sats still hit the victim's color through two ring beams at exactly the ring offset 5.0° = −7.16 dB each ⇒ per-sat relative interference 0.385·G₀ vs option (ii)'s 1.385·G₀ — only 5.6 dB/sat. Direct simulation with the repo gain function: 4-sat spread median SIR −6.0 dB (ii) vs −1.8 dB (i); 5-sat −5.9 vs −2.6 dB — a ~3-4 dB thinning, both regimes interference-dominated. The option table presents a clean dichotomy ('cleaner spectrum, weaker interference regime' vs 'strong') and L2-4 materiality would be re-measured against a mis-described contrast. Kept despite the known-incorporated 'D4b becomes consequential under D1 density' coupling because it shows the r2 option-description text itself is wrong.
/home/sat/route-c-wt/docs/research/env-foundation/07-design-coupling-findings.md:79:- **Mechanism:** D2b says 'activation driven by the assignment itself' but never says what happens when the joint action demands >K distinct beams on one satellite. Not a corner case: under the 28-slot head with 100 users at ε=1, expected distinct demanded beams ≈ 6.8/sat >> K=3 (still ~2 extra at ε=0.1); the per-user REP-012 mask CANNOT express the joint constraint (a beam's eligibility depends on other users' same-step actions). Each resolution couples somewhere: (a) same-step budget masking is undefined under simultaneous selection; previous-step-active-set masking introduces activation hysteresis = a new MDP state variable, and at most 4×K ∈ {12,16,20} of 28 actions stay live — eroding the reading-B always-full-catalog premise that justified the window head; (b) sequential admission by ue_id makes physics order-coupled — exactly the L3-6(b) failure class; (c) accept-then-bump via the existing fallback concentrates bumped users onto slot-0/beam-0 (load/interference artifact). Trainer-side: replay stores the CHOSEN action and trains Q(s,a_chosen) on the reward/next-state of whatever the env EXECUTED after repair/clip — systematic reward misattribution, not benign off-policy learning. Standard-side: L3-1's iff criterion ('0 violations over ≥10^5 UE-steps') is violated by construction whenever a UE on a deactivated-but-visible finite-SNR beam reads zero throughput — Amendment A1 added L3-7 without touching L3-1's wording, so a rebuilt env faithfully implementing D2b mechanically fails its own acceptance gate. The L3-7 planner spec ('mask-respecting assignment') silently presupposes the semantics this undefined rule was supposed to provide.
