/home/sat/route-c-wt/docs/research/env-foundation/07-design-coupling-findings.md:132:### [F20] D4's headline 'M1 drops ≈×3' does not survive the D5 noise shift it cites in the same line: the net rate effect is regime-dependent (÷1.46 noise-limited to ÷2.83 interference-dominated) and the regime is selected by D4b — a decision listed as open with no default — so L4-4/L4-5/L3-7 numeric expectations carry a ~2× ambiguity if derived from D4 alone
/home/sat/route-c-wt/docs/research/env-foundation/07-design-coupling-findings.md:135:- **Mechanism:** 04 D4 states 'M1 scale drops ≈×3' and 'noise bandwidth follows D5' in one consequence line, but they interact: switching σ² from N₀·B_full to N₀·W_u shrinks the noise floor by 10log10(3N)−NF ≈ 15.1 dB at N≈14.3, so SINR rises while rate-bandwidth shrinks ×3. On interference-free links (collapse cells, light beams, or a D4b(i)-leaning regime) the rate ratio is (1/3)·log2(1+839)/log2(1+25.8) = 0.682 ⇒ drop only ×1.46; only on interference-dominated links (live median degradation 9.95 dB) does it reach 0.353 ⇒ ×2.83 ≈ ×3. Which regime dominates is decided by D4b. Downstream, the L4-4 r1-scale targets, L4-5 expected observed ratio, and the QoS-floor margin feeding L3-7 inherit the 2× ambiguity.
